.TH TIME 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
time \- time a command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B time
.I command
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B time
runs
.I command
with its arguments, waits for it, and then writes how long it took to
standard error, after a blank line:
.PP
.nf
real    0m1.250s
.fi
.PP
.I command
is found as the shell finds it: a name with a
.B /
is a path, else the first match in
.BR PATH .
It runs with
.BR time 's
standard input, output and error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The command's exit status; 128 +
.I n
if a signal
.I n
ended it; 127 if it isn't found; 126 if it can't be started; 2 (with a
usage message) when no command is given.
.SH NOTES
Only the real (elapsed) time is reported, from the computer's uptime: the
kernel doesn't count user or system time. There are no options. It is a
program, not a shell keyword, so it times one command, not a pipeline.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR uptime (1),
.BR sleep (1)
